$ ==========================================================================
$ CaSrO.TDB  -  Ca-Sr-O system
$ --------------------------------------------------------------------------
$ Thermodynamic database for Thermo-Calc, condensed phases.
$ Liquid phase described with the ASSOCIATE MODEL (species Ca, Sr, O,
$ CaO, SrO).
$
$ Assembled 2026-08-03.  Syntax checked against the Database Manager
$ User Guide (Thermo-Calc Documentation Set, 2025a) and against PGEO.TDB.
$
$ SOURCES
$ [1] Deffrennes, Jakse, Alvares, Nuta, Pasturel, Khvan, Pisch,
$     "Thermodynamic modelling of the Ca-O system including 3rd
$     generation description of CaO and CaO2", Calphad 69 (2020) 101764.
$ [2] Risold, Hallstedt, Gauckler, "The strontium-oxygen system",
$     Calphad 20(3) (1996) 353-361.
$ [3] Risold, Hallstedt, Gauckler, "Thermodynamic modeling and calculation
$     of phase equilibria in the Sr-Ca-Cu-O system at ambient pressure",
$     J. Am. Ceram. Soc. 80(3) (1997) 537-548.  [Table VI]
$ [4] Jacob, Jayadevan, "Tie-lines and mixing properties of solid
$     solutions in the system CaO-SrO-PbO-O at 1100 K", J. Phase
$     Equilibria 21(4) (2000) 350-356.  [original data: Jacob and
$     Waseda, J. Am. Ceram. Soc. 81(4) (1998) 1065-1068]
$ [5] Aljarrah, Medraj, "Thermodynamic modelling of the Mg-Ca, Mg-Sr,
$     Ca-Sr and Mg-Ca-Sr systems using the modified quasichemical
$     model", Calphad 32 (2008) 240-251.
$ [6] Dinsdale, "SGTE data for pure elements", Calphad 15(4) (1991) 317.
$     Unaries copied verbatim from SGTE PURE5 (Thermo-Calc 2025a).
$
$ --------------------------------------------------------------------------
$ MODELLING NOTES  (read before use)
$
$ 1. GENERATION. The database is 2nd generation as far as the LIQUID
$    PHASE is concerned. The stoichiometric phases CaO and CaO2 keep the
$    3rd generation Einstein functions of [1]: being stoichiometric they
$    conflict with nothing and they fit Cp and S better than a
$    polynomial. They are written with the built-in Thermo-Calc function
$    GEIN(theta) = 1.5*R*theta + 3*R*T*LN(1-EXP(-theta/T)), documented in
$    the Database Manager User Guide.
$
$ 2. CaO LIQUID. [1] describes CaO(liq) with the two-state model, which
$    in Thermo-Calc is a property of the WHOLE PHASE and therefore cannot
$    coexist with a polynomial SrO(liq) inside the same associate phase.
$    It was refitted to classical SGTE form imposing Tm = 3222 K and
$    dHfus = 80.924 kJ/mol as exact constraints. It reproduces the
$    two-state curve with rms 186 J/mol over 1000-5000 K and keeps
$    G(liq) - G(cryst) > 0 down to 298 K (no restabilization).
$    Cp(liq) = 82.8 J/mol/K at Tm (Gurvich estimate: 84 +- 10).
$    Trade-off: the glass transition is lost, that is, METASTABLE liquid
$    CaO below ~1500 K no longer reproduces the rise in Cp on cooling.
$    Irrelevant for slag equilibria.
$
$ 3. Sr-O LIQUID. [2] uses the two-sublattice ionic liquid
$    (Sr+2)2(Va-2,O-2)2 with IDEAL mixing and a single cation. That case
$    is mathematically identical to an ideal associate solution
$    {Sr, SrO} (y_Va = x_Sr, y_O-2 = x_SrO), so the conversion is
$    algebraic, requires no reoptimization, and L(LIQUID,SR,SRO) = 0
$    exactly.
$
$ 4. CORRECTION TO [2]. The T*LN(T) coefficient of GSROCR is 47.56, NOT
$    47.36. With 47.36 the function gives S(298.15) = 52.27 and
$    dfH = -592.20 kJ/mol; with 47.56 it gives 53.61 and -592.14, which
$    are the values the paper itself reports in its Tables 2 and 3
$    (53.58 and -592.15). Checked against the original PDF.
$
$ 5. ESTIMATED, NOT MEASURED PARAMETERS -> treat with caution:
$      L(LIQUID,CAO,SRO;0) = 25000
$        From [3] Table VI: L(liq,Sr+2,Ca+2:O-2) = 50000 J per mole of
$        formula unit of the ionic liquid (Sr,Ca)2O2, that is 2 oxide
$        units -> 25000 J/mol of oxide in the associate model. The
$        authors of [3] themselves state that the mixing behaviour of the
$        SrO-CaO liquid is UNKNOWN and used that value as a fitting
$        parameter for melting temperatures in the quaternary.
$      L(LIQUID,SRO,O;0) = +30*T
$        Copied by analogy from the Ca-O value [1], which in turn comes
$        from Liang et al. There is no experimental datum in Sr-O; in
$        fact [2] does not extend the liquid beyond the SrO composition.
$      There are no cross parameters L(LIQUID,CA,SRO) or
$      L(LIQUID,SR,CAO): no information exists and they are left at the
$      default of zero.
$
$ 6. GAS PHASE. Included with O2 ONLY, which suffices to fix pO2 and to
$    compute the stability limits of CaO2 and SrO2 (in the oxygen-rich
$    region where decomposition occurs, [1] verifies that O2 dominates
$    and that any other species stays below 1e-29 atm). For high
$    temperature vaporization Ca(g), Ca2(g), O(g), O3(g), Sr(g), Sr2(g),
$    Sr2O(g), CaO(g) and SrO(g) are MISSING: they come from SSUB and are
$    NOT here. For the Ca-CaO liquidus at 1 bar, suspend GAS (as [1] does).
$
$ 7. The fcc and bcc metals do not dissolve oxygen, consistent with [1]
$    and [2]. Ca and Sr form continuous solid solutions in both [5].
$
$ 8. Effective range of validity: 298.15 to 3000 K, limited by the Sr and
$    SrO functions. Those of Ca-O reach 6000 K.
$ ==========================================================================

 ELEMENT /-  ELECTRON_GAS        0.0000E+00  0.0000E+00  0.0000E+00 !
 ELEMENT VA  VACUUM              0.0000E+00  0.0000E+00  0.0000E+00 !
 ELEMENT CA  FCC_A1              4.0078E+01  6.1965E+03  4.1589E+01 !
 ELEMENT O   1/2_MOLE_O2(GAS)    1.5999E+01  4.3410E+03  1.0252E+02 !
 ELEMENT SR  FCC_A1              8.7620E+01  0.0000E+00  0.0000E+00 !

 SPECIES CAO   CA1O1 !
 SPECIES SRO   SR1O1 !
 SPECIES O2    O2 !

$ ==========================================================================
$ SGTE UNARY FUNCTIONS (copied verbatim from PURE5, see [6])
$ ==========================================================================

 FUNCTION GHSERCA 298.15  -4955.062+72.794266*T-16.3138*T*LN(T)
   -11.10455E-3*T**2-133574*T**(-1);                        1115.00 Y
   -107304.428+799.982066*T-114.2922467*T*LN(T)
   +23.733814E-3*T**2-1.2438E-6*T**3+18245540*T**(-1);      3000.00 Y
   -3703.12+192.63995*T-35.0*T*LN(T);                       3001.00 N !

 FUNCTION GLIQCA  298.15  +5844.846+62.4838*T-16.3138*T*LN(T)
   -11.10455E-3*T**2-133574*T**(-1);                         500.00 Y
   +7838.856+18.2979*T-8.9874787*T*LN(T)
   -22.66537E-3*T**2+3.338303E-6*T**3-230193*T**(-1);       1115.00 Y
   -2654.938+188.9223*T-35*T*LN(T);                         3001.00 N !

 FUNCTION GBCCCA  298.15  -7020.852+142.970155*T-28.2541*T*LN(T)
   +7.2326E-3*T**2-4.500217E-6*T**3+60578*T**(-1);           716.00 Y
   +1640.475+1.999694*T-6.276*T*LN(T)
   -16.1921E-3*T**2-523000*T**(-1);                         1115.00 Y
   -142331.096+1023.549046*T-143.8726979*T*LN(T)
   +32.543127E-3*T**2-1.704079E-6*T**3+25353771*T**(-1);    3000.00 Y
   +321.63+189.433057*T-35*T*LN(T);                         3001.00 N !

 FUNCTION GHSERSR 298.15  -7532.367+107.183879*T-23.905*T*LN(T)
   -4.61225E-3*T**2-0.167477E-6*T**3-2055*T**(-1);           820.00 Y
   -13380.102+153.196104*T-30.0905432*T*LN(T)
   -3.251266E-3*T**2+0.184189E-6*T**3+850134*T**(-1);       3000.00 N !

 FUNCTION GLIQSR  298.15  +2194.997-10.118994*T-5.0668978*T*LN(T)
   -31.840595E-3*T**2+4.981237E-6*T**3-265559*T**(-1);      1050.00 Y
   -10855.29+213.406219*T-39.463*T*LN(T);                   3000.00 N !

 FUNCTION GBCCSR  298.15  -6779.234+116.583654*T-25.6708365*T*LN(T)
   -3.126762E-3*T**2+0.22965E-6*T**3+27649*T**(-1);          820.00 Y
   -6970.594+122.067301*T-26.57*T*LN(T)
   -1.9493E-3*T**2-0.017895E-6*T**3+16495*T**(-1);          1050.00 Y
   +8168.357+0.423037*T-9.7788593*T*LN(T)
   -9.539908E-3*T**2+0.520221E-6*T**3-2414794*T**(-1);      3000.00 N !

 FUNCTION GHSEROO 298.15  -3480.87-25.503038*T-11.1355*T*LN(T)
   -5.098875E-3*T**2+0.661845833E-6*T**3-38365*T**(-1);     1000.00 Y
   -6568.763+12.659879*T-16.8138*T*LN(T)
   -0.5957975E-3*T**2+0.006781E-6*T**3+262905*T**(-1);      3300.00 Y
   -13986.728+31.259624*T-18.9536*T*LN(T)
   -0.425243E-3*T**2+0.010721E-6*T**3+4383200*T**(-1);      6000.00 N !

 FUNCTION GLIQOO  298.15  +GHSEROO#-2648.9+31.44*T;         6000.00 N !

$ ==========================================================================
$ CRYSTALLINE CaO  -  [1] Table 1
$
$ Lower branch (Eq. 8, three Einstein temperatures):
$   G = E0 + SUM(ai*GEIN(ti)) - (a/2)*T**2 - EXP(b+c*T)/c**2
$   E0 = -652134.4
$   a1 = 1.142993  t1 = 369.447
$   a2 = 0.62542   t2 = 601.229
$   a3 = 0.218718  t3 = 188.291
$   a  = 0.00364916 -> a/2 = 0.00182458
$   b  = -15.0586   c = 0.00304142 -> 1/c**2 = 108105.354444
$
$ Upper branch (Eq. 10 of [1]), damping of Cp to keep the solid from
$ restabilizing at very high temperature. The original form
$   G = H' - S'*T + A*T*(1-LN(T)) + 1/(2*C**2*(B+C*T))
$   H' = -731248.3  S' = -492.1078  A = 81.5229
$   B  = -191.395   C  = 0.06241502 -> 1/(2*C**2) = 128.348789
$ CANNOT BE WRITTEN in a TDB: the parser rejects division by an
$ expression, and also the parenthesis of T*(1-LN(T)). It was refitted to
$ classical SGTE form over 3222-6000 K imposing EXACT continuity of G and
$ of S at 3222 K. Deviation from Eq. 10: maximum 14.8 J/mol, rms 8.2.
$ Resulting Cp between 80.9 and 82.2 J/mol/K, and the solid does not
$ restabilize against the liquid anywhere from 3222 to 6000 K.
$
$ Checked: S(298.15) = 40.354 (paper 40.35), Cp(298.15) = 42.730 (42.73),
$ dfH(298.15) = -634.601 kJ/mol (-634.6). The two branches join with a
$ 2 J/mol step in G, attributable to rounding in [1].
$
$ IMPORTANT - why this is split into auxiliary functions:
$ The Thermo-Calc TDB reader aborts with
$     ERROR 1261 IN GZCFUN: MISSING ) AFTER A LOG, EXP, GEIN or ECEIN
$ if a single expression accumulates several unary function calls. With
$ the three GEIN and the EXP together in one term, the parser truncates
$ the expression at 78 characters and is left with an unclosed GEIN(.
$ One call per function makes the problem disappear. The numerical result
$ is identical: GCAOE1+GCAOE2+GCAOE3 is the same Einstein sum.
$ ==========================================================================

$ The anharmonic term is factorized:  exp(b+c*T) = exp(b)*exp(c*T).
$ -108105.354444*exp(-15.0586) = -3.1187482541E-02
$ Checked: at 3222 K both forms give -562.120801 J/mol.

 FUNCTION GCAOE1  298.15  +1.142993*GEIN(369.447);          6000.00 N !
 FUNCTION GCAOE2  298.15  +0.62542*GEIN(601.229);           6000.00 N !
 FUNCTION GCAOE3  298.15  +0.218718*GEIN(188.291);          6000.00 N !
 FUNCTION GCAOANH 298.15  -3.1187482541E-02*EXP(0.00304142*T);
                                                            6000.00 N !

 FUNCTION GCAOCR  298.15  -652134.4+GCAOE1#+GCAOE2#+GCAOE3#
   -0.00182458*T**2+GCAOANH#;                              3222.00 Y
   -726686.2348+556.326025*T-79.464890*T*LN(T)
   -2.269506E-04*T**2;                                     6000.00 N !

$ ==========================================================================
$ LIQUID CaO  -  refitted in this work (see NOTE 2)
$ Exact constraints: Tm = 3221.99 K, dHfus = 80.924 kJ/mol
$ Cp(liq) = 84.371 - 4.748E-04*T  ->  82.8 J/mol/K at Tm
$ ==========================================================================

 FUNCTION GCAOLIQ 298.15  -656748.5662+572.751716*T
   -84.370735*T*LN(T)+2.374069E-04*T**2;                   6000.00 N !

$ ==========================================================================
$ CRYSTALLINE CaO2  -  [1] Table 1, Eq. 8, without the a, b, c terms
$   E0 = -676359.7
$   a1 = 2.04814  t1 = 398.195
$   a2 = 0.45973  t2 = 184.418
$   a3 = 0.56518  t3 = 954.326
$ Checked: S(298.15) = 59.600 (paper 59.60), Cp(298.15) = 61.627
$ (61.63), dfH(298.15) = -648.103 kJ/mol (-648.1)
$ ==========================================================================

$ Split into auxiliaries for the same reason as GCAOCR (see above).

 FUNCTION GCO2E1  298.15  +2.04814*GEIN(398.195);           6000.00 N !
 FUNCTION GCO2E2  298.15  +0.45973*GEIN(184.418);           6000.00 N !
 FUNCTION GCO2E3  298.15  +0.56518*GEIN(954.326);           6000.00 N !

 FUNCTION GCAO2CR 298.15  -676359.7+GCO2E1#+GCO2E2#+GCO2E3#;
                                                            6000.00 N !

$ ==========================================================================
$ SrO and SrO2  -  [2] Table 5  (corrected coefficient 47.56, see NOTE 4)
$ Checked: S(298.15) = 53.606 (paper 53.58), dfH = -592.143 kJ/mol
$ (-592.15), Tm = 2870.45 K (2870), dHfus = 89.408 kJ/mol (89.41),
$ Cp(liq) = 73.12 (73.1), dfH(SrO2) = -635.88 kJ/mol (-636)
$ ==========================================================================

 FUNCTION GSROCR  298.15  -607870+268.9*T-47.56*T*LN(T)
   -0.00307*T**2+190000*T**(-1);                           3000.00 N !

 FUNCTION GSROLIQ 298.15  -566346+449.0*T-73.1*T*LN(T);    6000.00 N !

 FUNCTION GSRO2CR 298.15  +GSROCR#+GHSEROO#-43740+70*T;    3000.00 N !

 TYPE_DEFINITION % SEQ *!
 DEFINE_SYSTEM_DEFAULT ELEMENT 2 !
 DEFAULT_COMMAND DEF_SYS_ELEMENT VA /- !

$ ==========================================================================
$ LIQUID PHASE - ASSOCIATE MODEL
$ ==========================================================================

 PHASE LIQUID:L % 1 1.0 !
 CONSTITUENT LIQUID:L :CA,CAO,O,SR,SRO: !

 PARAMETER G(LIQUID,CA;0)  298.15 +GLIQCA#;       3001.00 N REF6 !
 PARAMETER G(LIQUID,SR;0)  298.15 +GLIQSR#;       3000.00 N REF6 !
 PARAMETER G(LIQUID,O;0)   298.15 +GLIQOO#;       6000.00 N REF6 !
 PARAMETER G(LIQUID,CAO;0) 298.15 +GCAOLIQ#;      6000.00 N REF1 !
 PARAMETER G(LIQUID,SRO;0) 298.15 +GSROLIQ#;      6000.00 N REF2 !

$ Ca-CaO: accepted version of [1], fitted to the Fischbach solubilities.
$ Alternative of [1] using the Zaitsev activities: +9284
 PARAMETER L(LIQUID,CA,CAO;0)  298.15 -9625+12.30*T;
                                                 6000.00 N REF1 !

$ CaO-O: [1], taken in turn from Liang et al.
 PARAMETER L(LIQUID,CAO,O;0)   298.15 +30*T;      6000.00 N REF1 !

$ Sr-SrO: EXACTLY zero. The ionic liquid of [2] is ideal (NOTE 3).
 PARAMETER L(LIQUID,SR,SRO;0)  298.15 +0.0;       6000.00 N REF2 !

$ SrO-O: estimated by analogy with Ca-O (see NOTE 5).
 PARAMETER L(LIQUID,SRO,O;0)   298.15 +30*T;      6000.00 N REF7 !

$ CaO-SrO: from [3] Table VI, converted from ionic liquid to associates.
$ FITTING PARAMETER, NOT AN EXPERIMENTAL DATUM (see NOTE 5).
 PARAMETER L(LIQUID,CAO,SRO;0) 298.15 +25000;     6000.00 N REF3 !

$ Metallic Ca-Sr: [5], random solution model column.
 PARAMETER L(LIQUID,CA,SR;0)   298.15 +1682+0.59*T;
                                                 3000.00 N REF5 !
 PARAMETER L(LIQUID,CA,SR;1)   298.15 +521.05+0.052*T;
                                                 3000.00 N REF5 !
 PARAMETER L(LIQUID,CA,SR;2)   298.15 -900;       3000.00 N REF5 !

$ ==========================================================================
$ HALITE (Ca,Sr)O  -  continuous rock-salt solid solution, with a
$ miscibility gap at low temperature.
$
$ Excess parameters from [3] Table VI (phase 1x0):
$     L = 23000 - 3*T + 1185*(y_Ca - y_Sr)
$ Alternative derived in this work from the data of [4] (Hardy
$ subregular form converted to Redlich-Kister):
$     L0 = 25977.5 - 5.58*T    L1 = 1062.5 - 0.24*T
$ The two sets agree to within 0.7 % at 1100 K (19700 vs 19840). That of
$ [3] is adopted, being the published and assessed CALPHAD parameter.
$ Experimental gap at 1100 K [4]: x_SrO = 0.24 and 0.71.
$ ==========================================================================

 PHASE HALITE % 2 1 1 !
 CONSTITUENT HALITE :CA,SR : O: !

 PARAMETER G(HALITE,CA:O;0)    298.15 +GCAOCR#;   6000.00 N REF1 !
 PARAMETER G(HALITE,SR:O;0)    298.15 +GSROCR#;   3000.00 N REF2 !
 PARAMETER L(HALITE,CA,SR:O;0) 298.15 +23000-3*T;
                                                 3000.00 N REF3 !
 PARAMETER L(HALITE,CA,SR:O;1) 298.15 +1185;      3000.00 N REF3 !

$ ==========================================================================
$ STOICHIOMETRIC PEROXIDES
$ ==========================================================================

 PHASE CAO2 % 2 1 2 !
 CONSTITUENT CAO2 :CA : O: !
 PARAMETER G(CAO2,CA:O;0)      298.15 +GCAO2CR#;  6000.00 N REF1 !

 PHASE SRO2 % 2 1 2 !
 CONSTITUENT SRO2 :SR : O: !
 PARAMETER G(SRO2,SR:O;0)      298.15 +GSRO2CR#;  3000.00 N REF2 !

$ ==========================================================================
$ METALS  -  Ca and Sr form continuous solid solutions in fcc and bcc [5]
$ Parameters from [5], random solution column (J/mol of atoms)
$ ==========================================================================

 PHASE FCC_A1 % 2 1 1 !
 CONSTITUENT FCC_A1 :CA,SR : VA: !
 PARAMETER G(FCC_A1,CA:VA;0)   298.15 +GHSERCA#;  3001.00 N REF6 !
 PARAMETER G(FCC_A1,SR:VA;0)   298.15 +GHSERSR#;  3000.00 N REF6 !
 PARAMETER L(FCC_A1,CA,SR:VA;0) 298.15 +3770.03+0.11*T;
                                                 3000.00 N REF5 !

 PHASE BCC_A2 % 2 1 3 !
 CONSTITUENT BCC_A2 :CA,SR : VA: !
 PARAMETER G(BCC_A2,CA:VA;0)   298.15 +GBCCCA#;   3001.00 N REF6 !
 PARAMETER G(BCC_A2,SR:VA;0)   298.15 +GBCCSR#;   3000.00 N REF6 !
 PARAMETER L(BCC_A2,CA,SR:VA;0) 298.15 +3770.03+0.01*T;
                                                 3000.00 N REF5 !

$ ==========================================================================
$ GAS  -  O2 ONLY (see NOTE 6). Incomplete on purpose.
$ ==========================================================================

 PHASE GAS:G % 1 1.0 !
 CONSTITUENT GAS:G :O2: !
 PARAMETER G(GAS,O2;0) 298.15 +2*GHSEROO#+R*T*LN(1.0E-05*P);
                                                 6000.00 N REF6 !

$ ==========================================================================

$ Jacob, Jayadevan, J Phase Equilibria 21(4) (2000) 350-356 is the source
$ of the experimental gap data used in CaSrO.POP, not of any parameter of
$ this database. That is why it does not appear in the reference list.
 LIST_OF_REFERENCES
 NUMBER  SOURCE
  REF1 'Deffrennes et al., Calphad 69 (2020) 101764'
  REF2 'Risold, Hallstedt, Gauckler, Calphad 20(3) (1996) 353-361'
  REF3 'Risold et al., J Am Ceram Soc 80(3) (1997) 537-548, Table VI'
  REF5 'Aljarrah, Medraj, Calphad 32 (2008) 240-251'
  REF6 'Dinsdale, Calphad 15(4) (1991) 317-425 (SGTE PURE5)'
  REF7 'Estimated in this work by analogy with Ca-O'
 !
